{
  "script": [
    {
      "beatId": "beat1",
      "narration": "In March 2025, Larkspur Pay checkout failed for forty-seven minutes. A rate limiter, the gate on every request, let thirty-one percent of checkouts fail.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Rate limiter = may you go now?",
        "semantic": "concept"
      },
      "tags": [
        "checkout failing",
        "too much traffic"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "31%",
          "label": "Checkouts that failed",
          "sourceRef": "num-15"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Post-incident report: checkout errors on 14 March 2025"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "beat2",
      "narration": "Each checkout request gets a key and a token bucket, a pile of passes. Say the bucket holds a hundred tokens. It refills ten a second; each request spends one.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Token bucket = a pile of passes",
        "semantic": "concept"
      },
      "tags": [
        "per-key bucket",
        "spend one token"
      ]
    },
    {
      "beatId": "beat3",
      "narration": "So why not give each gateway node its own bucket? Across, say, twenty-four nodes, a client gets twenty-four times its limit. So one shared store holds the bucket, one place every node reads and writes.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Shared store = one bucket, all nodes",
        "semantic": "concept"
      },
      "tags": [
        "bucket per node",
        "shared store"
      ]
    },
    {
      "beatId": "beat4",
      "narration": "But a shared bucket needs read, refill and write as one step. Otherwise two nodes read the same count and both see a token left. Both let their request through.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Two nodes spend one token",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "double spend"
      ]
    },
    {
      "beatId": "beat5",
      "narration": "But the bucket lives across the network. What if the store gets slow? A round trip is zero point four milliseconds at the median. At p ninety-nine, two point one.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Every check crosses the network",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "network round trip",
        "slow store"
      ],
      "source": "A field guide to token-bucket rate limiting"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "beat6",
      "narration": "Then a routine failover moved that store to a replica in another zone. Store calls went from one millisecond to eighty milliseconds. Those calls blew past the timeout, so every check counted as a failure.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "A failover breaks the timeout",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "cross-zone replica",
        "past the timeout"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "80 ms",
          "label": "Store call after failover",
          "sourceRef": "num-16"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Post-incident report: checkout errors on 14 March 2025"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "beat7",
      "narration": "So with the store slow, the limiter let requests through uncounted. Each of the forty checkout instances enforced the limit alone. Traffic reached the payment provider at roughly forty times its contracted rate. Checkouts started failing.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Each instance enforces its own limit",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "uncounted traffic",
        "local limits"
      ],
      "source": "Larkspur Pay platform team incident report, 14 Mar 2025"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "beat8",
      "narration": "The payment limiter now refuses what it can't count. If the store fails, each instance gets its share of the contract. The timeout rose to a hundred and fifty milliseconds, longer than a cross-zone trip. So that checkout waits, or is refused.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Refuse what you can't count",
        "semantic": "resolution"
      },
      "tags": [
        "refuse uncounted",
        "split fallback"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "150 ms",
          "label": "Store timeout after fix",
          "sourceRef": "num-20"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Larkspur Pay platform team incident report, 14 Mar 2025"
    }
  ]
}

